    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d143">

                <pb facs="0127" n="123" xml:id="pg123"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102S/1589: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Consul General at
                        Shanghai</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-01-14T20:00:00-05:00">January 14, 1938—8
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>41. Reference your 64, January 12, 1 p.m.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>The Department is inclined to share the views expressed in paragraph 6 of
                        your telegram under reference and authorizes you to acquaint the British
                        Chargé d’Affaires with those views and with the substance of the
                        Department’s 28, January 11, 6 p.m., to you. You may, in your discretion,
                        inform him that if the British insist upon adherence to the course outlined
                        in the exchange of telegrams described in your 64, we will refrain from any
                        action that might embarrass them.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>With reference to paragraph 5 of your telegram, you may use this
                        instruction and the Department’s 28 as a basis for advising the Americans on
                        the Council.</item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
